20:01:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is completely unsurprising that you have never seen one from headlights or ordinary lamps. The conditions are much harder to meet than for a solar rainbow. But when they do occur, they are the same optical phenomenon—not just “something colorful.” A true primary rainbow is identified by a specific geometry: light is refracted into spherical water droplets, internally reflected once, and refracted out again. Red light emerges most strongly at about **42°** from the direction opposite the source, with violet at about **40°**. That happens whether the source is the Sun, Moon, a white floodlight, or a sufficiently bright white lamp. Why the Sun produces familiar rainbows and lamps usually do not: - **Brightness and coverage:** The Sun illuminates billions of droplets across a huge section of sky. A headlight illuminates a narrow, nearby cone of rain or spray. - **Viewing geometry:** For a bow, the light must be behind or very near the observer’s line of sight, while droplets are in front. Most street lamps are off to one side or overhead. - **Artificial light patterns:** Headlights are aimed toward the road, often with sharply shaped beams, not broadly into airborne rain at the needed angles. - **Night vision:** At low light levels, human color vision becomes weak, so even a real bow may look pale or whitish. - **Light spectrum:** Some lamps—especially older sodium streetlights—produce nearly one color, so they cannot make a vivid multicolored spectrum. The Moon provides a useful real-world comparison: **moonbows** are genuine rainbows made by moonlight, but most people have never seen one because they require a bright near-full Moon, dark surroundings, rain in the right place, and an unobstructed view. They often look white to the eye even though cameras reveal their colors. You can make an artificial rainbow more deliberately with a strong white light and fine water spray, such as a powerful flashlight or floodlight illuminating a sprinkler or mist at night. The source should be close to or behind you, and the visible bow will appear roughly 40–42° away from the forward direction. It may be only a small arc or part of a circle because the lamp lights only part of the mist. So: lamps do not *usually* produce visible rainbows in everyday situations, but they absolutely can produce the same refraction-and-internal-reflection rainbow physics as sunlight.
