20:02:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a reasonable language distinction to make. In everyday speech, **“a rainbow” usually means the natural arc seen after rain, with the Sun behind you**. In that sense, saying “rainbows are made by sunlight” is perfectly normal shorthand. In optics, though, *rainbow* refers to the particular pattern produced when light enters spherical droplets, reflects once inside, and exits at color-dependent angles. Under that definition, the light source does not have to be the Sun. People may call the artificial case an **artificial-light rainbow**, **sprinkler rainbow**, or sometimes simply a **rainbow produced by a lamp**. It is not merely any spectrum made by water. A prism, a CD, or an oil slick can split light into colors, but they do not make a rainbow in the optical sense. The lamp-and-droplet case has the same characteristic angular concentration of rays—about 40–42° from the antisolar/anti-light point—and can have the same color order, secondary bow, and even supernumerary bands under suitable conditions. So there are two valid uses: - **Ordinary meaning:** a natural, Sun-and-rain weather phenomenon. - **Scientific/optical meaning:** a droplet-produced bow that can be illuminated by the Sun, Moon, or artificial broad-spectrum light. Calling the second one a rainbow is a technical extension of the everyday word, but not an arbitrary one: it is defined by the same physical mechanism and geometry.
